Will you come to the aid of the sentients who subsist in the
Low-Canals Region of Wâhaz? Will you pray for them?... 
• from Omnium Planetarum, Papae Iokannis XXIIIX, date IX.41.847.111

What is Wâhaz like?... 

Winds howl and scrape with abrasive dust of heavy metals. Your
spaceship makes planetfall and Low-Canal women called greeters or
/salvetators/ in the actually vulgar Latin of the natives coo the
greeting /Salve/ at you as you debark and it's the only Latin they
know. A planet well below the minimally habitable line according to
any sane judgment. Too small, too cold. A planet where nightrunners
thieve the contents of larders and cradles and leave the front door
swinging as they are gone into the darkling waste. The native homin
gather in heaps of hovels they are pleased to call towns, cities.
Manufactoria glow and burn and smoke day and night, in subarctic cold
and summer warmth. And through the long cool Spring of the year and
the long temperate Summer the long again Cool autumn too the
latifundia are crawling with figures stooping to the red earth doing
all the punishing labor. The people march homeward like armies of
irregulars utterly spent from some universal cataclysm of the world.
The huge sails of the iceboats dart like brightfeathered birds down
the wide and frozen canals that converge here, they sail straight as
the light of a holophote, gathering astounding speed, the only thing
visible that is not dreary, as they vanish with distance around the
foreshortened curvature of this small world. Flat terrain of red dust
maculated with green, low cities of red and grey brick, small hills
that disappear when the air gets dusty, and scrannel trees looking
metallic and skeletal standing against the cold gails. Feel them trembling as
they will themselves onward, by some mindless vegetal method or other,
into wilful persistence. The people turn to subspace radio dramas and drugs
and vox stations reticulum stations and strange sex and stranger sex
and a religion old as the homin species (as was commonly thought, and
as is commonly thought, for the present time of the story is the now
of the teller) for stimulation and escape, and all are available for
just a little money. Escape is imperative here, especially when
leaving is impossible.

See the man, he is dressed in black leather like a spacer, like he
just made planetfall and stepped straight into this tavern or bar. The
poisonous gases of climates of hot and alien seem to reek and threaten
from his very clothing. His cheeks are hollow, his eyes crazed and
bright and empty, and he is drinking heavily and without pleasure.
Next to him is a heavier unit, a homin female burlier than him (and he
came from Skâr by the look of him, also judging from where we /are/,
mythical old Yrth itself is the next planet away!) -- not a pretty
woman next to him, not gracile like a Wâhazene. Next to /her/ is a
starved addict, actually a Wâhazene to judge by her appearance, as we
seem wont to do here. She is snorting /foifir/ from an iridium mirror
laid upon the wooden table. She raises herself up, red hair flying.
--"That's good, that's better," she says and wipes her lovely,
trifling nose.

... and now a gentleman who is surely a scholar in the local academe
is growing drunken and blithely prolix on the subject of the latest
Date-of-Easter controversy, a large and Gordian affair considering
that here on Wâhaz it's based on the motions in outerspace of an alien
world and how best to translate those motions onto the surface and
into the lives of the faithful of Wâhaz, and considering also that no
matter the final decision few will care and those who still object to
it will be implacable in their hatred of the practice for the rest
of their lives. So says the gentleman scholar. It is not occuring to
him that these people at the next table -- the man and the two women
-- probably do not attend Mass at any Holy Martian Catholic Church.

A lanky barbarian nearby got his feelings hurt, no one knew how. No
one knew him. But the gentleman scholar went home with a concussion
and a bloody scalp. No doubt he thanked his God, because the man in
black leather lay supine upon the floor, perfectly still with a
steaming holophote hole in his chest. Maybe the grii doctors could
bring him back, if they ever got to him... The barbarian had his gun
taken away and was led off in chains by a magistrate and his
cataphract. No doubt he contemplated a lifetime of forced labor and
having no choices at all forever.  

The burly Skârkin woman had vanished, leaving only the gracile
maybe-Wâhazkin female from the original party, only a rather attractive
woman who was somewhat gracile but fundamentally unplaceable, dressed for
fun, here, in the Low-Canals Region, in the city Vernai, but this
woman could be from anywhere. 

The woman, now alone, was me. My dates for the night -- one was
prudently elsewhere and the other had a hole in his chest, and the
party was most definitely over for me. I'm the one who looked like a
/foifir/ addict, because at that time I was one. 

My name is... My policy in life, as of now, my personal philosophy, my
personal /mythlogy/, demands that I let it all out. That's what you're
reading. I want to hide nothing. To tell all. Starting now. Here it is. This
how I came to Wâhaz and what happened to me there.
